meta {
  name: Pressure CPU (PSI)
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/metrics/pressure/cpu?start={{metrics_start}}&end={{metrics_end}}&resolution={{metrics_resolution}}&limit={{metrics_limit}}
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

params:query {
  ~start: {{metrics_start}}
  ~end: {{metrics_end}}
  ~resolution: {{metrics_resolution}}
  ~limit: {{metrics_limit}}
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{jwt_token}}
}

docs {
  Linux 4.20+ Pressure Stall Information for CPU. Each point reports
  what fraction of time at least one task was stalled waiting for CPU
  over 10s / 60s / 300s windows.

  `some_*` covers any task being stalled; `full_*` requires every
  runnable task to be stalled. The kernel does not emit `full` on
  `cpu`, so those columns stay 0.0 for this resource.

  Sustained `some_avg60 > 10%` is a credible signal that the host
  cannot keep up with offered CPU work — either the workload is too
  heavy for the box or the VPS is being throttled.

  On non-Linux hosts (or pre-4.20 kernels) the underlying table simply
  has no rows; the response is `points: []` rather than an error.
}
